{"name":"Poseidon’s Beard","hex":"#d9d6c7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"poseidon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A specific name species descriptor. Only used in binomial names.","Used elliptically for the full binomial name."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/poseidon"},{"term":"beard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Facial hair on the chin, cheeks, jaw and neck.","The cluster of small feathers at the base of the beak in some birds."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To grow hair on the chin and jaw.","To boldly and bravely oppose or confront, often to the chagrin of the one being bearded."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beard","blocks":[{"text":"A beard is the hair that grows on the jaw, chin, upper lip, lower lip, cheeks, and neck of humans and some non-human animals. In humans, beards are most common among pubescent and adult males, though some women also develop them in a condition called hirsutism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Photography-wallpaper-facial-hair-man-beard-chin-moustache-human-elder-portrait-1453095.jpg/330px-Photography-wallpaper-facial-hair-man-beard-chin-moustache-human-elder-portrait-1453095.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beard","wikidata":"Q42804"},{"title":"Poseidon","blocks":[{"text":"Poseidon is one of the twelve Olympians in ancient Greek religion and mythology, presiding over the sea, storms, earthquakes and horses. He was the protector of seafarers and the guardian of many Hellenic cities and colonies. In pre-Olympian Bronze Age Greece, Poseidon was venerated as a chief deity at Pylos and Thebes, with the cult title \"earth shaker\"; in the myths of isolated Arcadia, he is related to Demeter and Despoina and was venerated as a horse, and as a god of the waters. Poseidon maintained both associations among most Greeks: he was regarded as the tamer or father of horses, who, with a strike of his trident, created springs. His Roman equivalent is Neptune."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Statue_of_Poseidon_NAMA_235_%28DerHexer%29%2C_part_2.JPG/330px-Statue_of_Poseidon_NAMA_235_%28DerHexer%29%2C_part_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poseidon","wikidata":"Q41127"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Baerte_ohne_text.jpg/500px-Baerte_ohne_text.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Baerte_ohne_text.jpg/500px-Baerte_ohne_text.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different types of beards: 1) Incipient 2) Moustache 3) Goatee or Mandarin 4) Spanish-style 5) Long sideburns 6) Sideburns joined by a moustache 7) Style Van Dyke 8) Full beard.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABaerte_ohne_text.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Anthropoid_sarcophagus_discovered_at_Cadiz_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_15052.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Anthropoid_sarcophagus_discovered_at_Cadiz_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_15052.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Phoenicians gave great attention to the beard, as can be seen in their sculptures.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnthropoid_sarcophagus_discovered_at_Cadiz_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_15052.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Black_Obelisk%2C_Jewish_delegation_to_Shalmaneser_III.jpg/500px-Black_Obelisk%2C_Jewish_delegation_to_Shalmaneser_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or